<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">André Masséna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Andre Massena</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">André Masséna was a French general of the Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, made a Marshal of the Empire in 1804. He won the Second Battle of Zurich in 1799, held besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> for two months in 1800, and fought at Aspern-Essling and Wagram in 1809. His invasion of Portugal was halted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lines-of-torres-vedras">Lines of Torres Vedras</a> in 1810–11.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andre-massena.jpg" alt="André Masséna" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edme-Adolphe Fontaine / After Antoine-Jean Gros · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andr%C3%A9_Mass%C3%A9na_(Fontaine_et_Gros).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>André Masséna was born Andrea Massena on 6 May 1758 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, then part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, the son of Jules César Masséna, a wine merchant, and Catherine Fabre. His father's family came originally from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and had lived for at least three centuries in the Vésubie valley. His father died of tuberculosis in 1764, and his mother, who quickly remarried, entrusted the children to relatives of her first husband. André was the eldest of three sons and was six years old. He began as an enlisted soldier under the old regime and rose without a formal military education.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the Revolutionary Wars Masséna served in the Army of the Var in the occupation of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> in 1792, and helped suppress the barbet movement that resisted it. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1796 he commanded a division; he and the other divisional commanders were at first sceptical of the younger general but were soon won over. Napoleon later wrote that Masséna was extraordinarily courageous and firm and ready to fight again after a defeat.</p><p>In 1799 he commanded the Army of Helvetia against Austrian and Russian forces. He was driven from Zurich by Archduke Charles on 4 to 6 June, entrenched behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limmat">Limmat</a>, and on 25 September defeated the Austro-Russians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Korsakov</a> at the Second Battle of Zurich, a victory with considerable strategic effect for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He opposed some of Bonaparte's political choices, but received the baton of Marshal of the Empire on 19 May 1804. In 1809 he commanded IV Corps, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> rewarded him with the titles Duke of Rivoli and Prince of Essling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 17 April 1810 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> gave Masséna the Army of Portugal for a third French invasion of that country, after the failed attempts of Junot and Soult. He had told the Emperor he had little enthusiasm for the task. Short of supplies, with unstable communications and subordinates who did not cooperate, he could muster only about 60,000 men to face <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>. The failure earned him the Emperor's disgrace, and he received no further major command in the Empire.</p><p>He lived at Rueil writing his memoirs, which were in fact written by General Jean-Baptiste-Frédéric Koch. In 1813 he commanded the 8th military division at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>. He rallied to the Bourbon Restoration and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 4 April 1817. His reputation for greed and plunder earned him the nickname the rotten child of victory, set against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s the dear child of victory, and most French marshals of the time served under his command at some point.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “André Masséna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1799 Zürich was a battlefield in the War of the Second Coalition, when French forces under <b>André Masséna</b> fought Austrian and then Russian armies in the two Battles of Zürich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1800 the Austrians and British besieged Genoa, held by General <b>Masséna</b>, causing terrible famine before the city surrendered shortly before Marengo restored French dominance.</blockquote><a href="/state/ligurian-republic">Ligurian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.7,7.27&amp;year=1796" aria-label="Nice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4725 615 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of André Masséna"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5175 885h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5175 885h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.7,7.27&amp;year=1796">What was here in 1796? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
